"""MediSimplify report content, from the Introduction to the References.

Markup inside text: **bold**, //italic//, `code`. Every number is taken from the project's
evaluation results (evaluation/results/2026-10-27-validator-v2) or its test suite.
"""

INTRO = [
    "Medical laboratory reports are written for clinicians. A typical report lists dozens of test names, abbreviations, numeric values, units, reference ranges and flags such as “H” or “L”, but gives no explanation of what each test measures or what a value outside the printed range might mean. Patients increasingly receive these reports directly through laboratory portals and messaging apps, often before they can speak to a doctor, and many turn to search engines or general-purpose AI chatbots for an explanation.",
    "Large language models (LLMs) can rewrite technical text in plain language, but used naively they introduce new risks: they may change a value or a unit, invent a test, state or imply a diagnosis, recommend treatment, or falsely reassure the reader that everything is normal [5, 7, 8]. The central question of this project is whether careful prompt engineering, combined with retrieval of trusted reference material and automatic output validation, can make LLM-generated explanations of lab reports faithful, grounded and safe enough to be useful as an educational aid.",
    "MediSimplify accepts a digitally generated PDF lab report and processes it in four auditable stages. It extracts every test result exactly as printed, retrieves background passages from a curated library of public-domain MedlinePlus pages, asks Google Gemini for a structured, cited explanation, and validates that explanation against the report and the retrieved sources before it is shown. Three prompting strategies—zero-shot, few-shot and retrieval-augmented (RAG)—are implemented under identical conditions so that they can be compared fairly.",
    "Three principles guide the design: faithfulness (values, units and ranges are never altered or invented), grounding (general medical background must be cited to a trusted source), and privacy (only structured test results are sent to the model; names, dates and report text stay on the server). MediSimplify is an educational prototype evaluated on synthetic reports; it is not a diagnostic tool and does not replace professional medical advice.",
]

ABSTRACT = [
    "Patients often receive laboratory reports that they cannot interpret, and general-purpose chatbots that explain them may alter values, invent facts or give unsafe advice. This report presents MediSimplify, a web application that converts PDF lab reports into patient-friendly summaries using prompt engineering and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), with automatic validation of every generated summary.",
    "A rule-based parser extracts each test name, value, unit, reference range and laboratory flag verbatim from the PDF text layer, including column-style tables and ranges printed below a result. General background is retrieved from 20 public-domain MedlinePlus pages split into 226 passages, embedded locally with Sentence Transformers (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) and stored in ChromaDB. A calibrated similarity threshold of 0.60 ensures that tests the library does not cover receive no evidence rather than a weak match.",
    "Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite generates a structured JSON summary under three prompting strategies that share the same instructions, report facts, schema and settings (temperature 0.2, seed 42). A validator then checks that values are copied exactly, that every citation refers to a retrieved passage, that abnormal results are supported by the report, and that the text contains no diagnostic, treatment or falsely reassuring language.",
    "On 12 synthetic evaluation cases, extraction matched all 34 gold results exactly and retrieval chose the correct reference page for 29 of 30 covered tests. In a live comparison on 9 reports, all three strategies preserved every value and produced no unsafe wording; zero-shot added unsourced background to all 34 explanations, few-shot added none, and RAG explained 30 of 34 tests with citations, 95% of which pointed to the correct page. On a frozen held-out set, the redesigned safety detector caught 57 of 60 paraphrased unsafe sentences, up from 3.",
    "**Keywords: **prompt engineering, retrieval-augmented generation, large language models, patient-friendly summaries, medical laboratory reports, few-shot prompting, output validation, Gemini.",
]

