CONCLUSION = [
    "This report has shown that a prompt-engineered, retrieval-augmented pipeline can turn supported PDF lab reports into structured, plain-language explanations that keep every reported value exact and cite trusted sources. The key design decision was to treat the language model as one controlled component: extraction is deterministic and verbatim, retrieval is restricted to an approved public-domain corpus with a calibrated relevance threshold, the model is constrained by shared instructions and a JSON schema, and every summary is validated before it is shown.",
    "The comparison of prompting strategies under identical conditions gave a clear, if descriptive, result. All three strategies preserved values and avoided unsafe wording in the live run. Zero-shot prompting ignored the instruction to omit unsourced background; few-shot prompting passed every automatic check but explained very little; and RAG-enhanced prompting was the only strategy that explained each test with citations a reader can verify, at about five times the prompt cost. This supports the use of retrieval for grounded patient education, and it shows that passing automatic checks must be read alongside informativeness.",
    "The work also has clear limitations. The parser supports digitally generated reports with one-line or column layouts and does not perform OCR; stratified ranges are shown but not compared; the safety validator is rule-based and its held-out result of 57 of 60 was measured on sentences written by the same author as the development set; the evaluation uses 12 synthetic cases and a single run; and the gold annotations and summaries have not yet been reviewed by independent human raters.",
    "Future work will collect blinded human ratings of accuracy, clarity, completeness and safety, build an independently written held-out safety set, add OCR and further report layouts, extend the reference corpus, and investigate a natural-language-inference check of whether each cited passage actually supports the sentence that cites it. MediSimplify is released as an open, reproducible prototype for education and research, not as a medical device.",
]

